### The platform, across service boundaries.
###
### Start both databases and both services first:
###   docker compose up -d                                    (from 02-bookstore-capstone/)
###   cd bookstore-platform/user-service && ../mvnw spring-boot:run
###   cd bookstore-platform/book-service && ../mvnw spring-boot:run
###
### The point of this file is what the per-service tests deliberately cannot cover: a token minted by
### user-service on port 8081 being accepted by book-service on port 8082, without the two ever
### talking to each other.

### FROM STEP 8, THE ONLY ADDRESS A CLIENT NEEDS IS THE GATEWAY.
###
### The per-service variables below still work and are kept deliberately: several requests at the end
### of this file BYPASS the gateway on purpose, to demonstrate that the services do not trust anything
### the gateway tells them. In a deployed platform 8081-8086 are not reachable from outside at all.

@gateway = http://localhost:8080

@userService = http://localhost:8081
@bookService = http://localhost:8082

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### user-service (8081) — identity
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 1. Register a customer -> 201
POST {{userService}}/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "shopper",
  "email": "shopper@example.com",
  "password": "correct-horse-battery"
}

### 2. Log in as that customer
# @name userLogin
POST {{userService}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "shopper", "password": "correct-horse-battery" }

@userToken = {{userLogin.response.body.token}}

### 3. Log in as the seeded dev admin
# @name adminLogin
POST {{userService}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "admin", "password": "admin-dev-password" }

@adminToken = {{adminLogin.response.body.token}}

### 4. The current user -> 200
GET {{userService}}/api/auth/me
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### book-service (8082) — catalog
###
### Every request below crosses a service boundary: the token came from 8081 and
### is being verified on 8082. Nothing on this port can read the users table, and
### no call is made back to user-service. Only the shared signing key is involved.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 5. Browsing is public — no token at all -> 200
GET {{bookService}}/api/books

### 6. Authors with their books, one query -> 200
GET {{bookService}}/api/authors

### 7. A customer creating a book -> 403 (authenticated on another service, still not permitted here)
POST {{bookService}}/api/books
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Not Allowed", "isbn": "NOPE-1", "price": 9.99, "stock": 1 }

### 8. The same request with the admin's token -> 201
POST {{bookService}}/api/books
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Cross-Service Book", "isbn": "XS-1", "price": 19.99, "stock": 5 }

### 9. Purchasing with the customer's token -> 200
POST {{bookService}}/api/books/1/purchase
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 1 }

### 10. Purchasing anonymously -> 401
POST {{bookService}}/api/books/1/purchase
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 1 }

### 11. A token from 8081 is useless if tampered with — edit one character of the
###     payload below by hand and this returns 401 rather than acting on it.
GET {{bookService}}/api/books/1
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### order-service (8083) — Step 5b
###
### Placing an order is the first request that fans out: order-service calls
### book-service over HTTP, forwarding the customer's token, before it can write
### anything of its own.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@orderService = http://localhost:8083

### 12. Place an order -> 201. Watch order-service's log: a Feign call per book.
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "items": [
    { "bookId": 1, "quantity": 2 },
    { "bookId": 2, "quantity": 1 }
  ]
}

### 13. My orders -> 200, scoped by the token rather than by any parameter
GET {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 14. Every order -> 403 for a customer
GET {{orderService}}/api/orders/all
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 15. Every order -> 200 for an admin
GET {{orderService}}/api/orders/all
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 16. A book that does not exist -> 404 (book-service's 404, retold)
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 99999, "quantity": 1 } ] }

### 17. More than the shelf holds -> 409, refused before any stock is reserved
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 1, "quantity": 99999 } ] }

### 18. Cancel an order -> 200
PUT {{orderService}}/api/orders/1/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### 19. THE INTERESTING ONE. Stop book-service, then run this.
###
###     -> 503, in about 100 ms
###
### Not 500: the request was fine and the catalog is down, which is what 503
### means. And note what still works with the catalog stopped — request 13 above
### returns order history in full, because the title and price were captured at
### order time rather than fetched on read.
###
### What is still wrong: every attempt pays the full timeout before failing, and
### one slow catalog can therefore exhaust order-service's threads. Step 5c.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 1, "quantity": 1 } ] }

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 5c — the circuit breaker, live
###
### 20-24 are a demonstration to run in order with book-service STOPPED, then
### restarted. Watch the state change between each.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 20. Circuit state. CLOSED while the catalog is healthy.
GET {{orderService}}/actuator/circuitbreakers

### 21. Stop book-service, then run this five or six times.
###     The first few take ~750 ms (three retries each) and return 503.
###     Once five calls have accumulated and half have failed, the circuit OPENS.
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 1, "quantity": 1 } ] }

### 22. State again -> OPEN, failureRate 60%
GET {{orderService}}/actuator/circuitbreakers

### 23. Order again -> still 503, but in ~85 ms instead of ~750 ms.
###     No request leaves the process at all; the remaining time is
###     order-service's own overhead.
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 1, "quantity": 1 } ] }

### 24. Restart book-service, wait 11 seconds (wait-duration-in-open-state: 10s),
###     then order again. The state goes OPEN -> HALF_OPEN -> CLOSED as the
###     three permitted probes succeed.
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 1, "quantity": 1 } ] }

### 25. Every state transition, with timestamps
GET {{orderService}}/actuator/circuitbreakerevents

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 5d — idempotency and the saga
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 26. Create a book with plenty of stock (admin), to demonstrate on
POST {{bookService}}/api/books
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "title": "Idempotency Demo", "isbn": "IDEM-1", "price": 10.00, "stock": 100 }

### 27. Reserve 10 with a fixed id. Run this THREE times.
###     Stock drops once, from 100 to 90, and stays there — every repeat is
###     recognised as the same intent rather than a new one.
POST {{bookService}}/api/books/7/purchase
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 10, "reservationId": "11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111" }

### 28. The same call WITHOUT an id. Run this three times too.
###     90 -> 80 -> 70 -> 60. This is what a retry used to do, and why 5c
###     refused to retry the write at all.
POST {{bookService}}/api/books/7/purchase
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "quantity": 10 }

### 29. Compensate: give the reservation back. Run twice — the second is a no-op,
###     because a compensating action gets retried at least as often as the
###     action it compensates.
POST {{bookService}}/api/books/reservations/11111111-1111-1111-1111-111111111111/release
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 30. Place an order and watch order-service's log:
###       "Order N created as PENDING with 1 reservations to place"
###     appears BEFORE any call to book-service. That ordering is the saga.
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 7, "quantity": 2 } ] }

### 31. The order comes back AWAITING_PAYMENT, and its line carries the
###     reservation id that book-service recorded. Compensation needs no guessing.
GET {{orderService}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 32. Cancel it. Stock is released BEFORE the status changes, so a crash in
###     between leaves an order that is still cancellable.
PUT {{orderService}}/api/orders/9/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### payment-service (8084) — Step 5e
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@paymentService = http://localhost:8084

### 33. Pay for an AWAITING_PAYMENT order -> 201.
###     payment-service reads the order from order-service (forwarding your
###     token), charges, records, and then tells order-service it was paid.
POST {{paymentService}}/api/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "orderId": 10 }

### 34. Pay again. Same payment id, no second charge — order_id is UNIQUE, and
###     that constraint is the whole duplicate-charge defence.
POST {{paymentService}}/api/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "orderId": 10 }

### 35. The order is now PAID
GET {{orderService}}/api/orders/10
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 36. Payment status for an order
GET {{paymentService}}/api/payments/10
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 37. An order that is not awaiting payment -> 409
POST {{paymentService}}/api/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "orderId": 10 }

### 38. A simulated decline. Order a book priced so the total ends in .13 and pay
###     for it -> 409, and the FAILED attempt is still recorded.
POST {{paymentService}}/api/payments
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{ "orderId": 11 }

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### 39. THE ROLL-FORWARD DEMO.
###
### Pay for an order, then simulate the crash where the money moved but the
### notification never landed:
###
###   docker exec bookstore-payment-db psql -U paymentdb -d paymentdb \
###     -c "UPDATE payment SET order_notified=FALSE WHERE order_id=12"
###   docker exec bookstore-order-db psql -U orderdb -d orderdb \
###     -c "UPDATE orders SET status='AWAITING_PAYMENT' WHERE id=12"
###
### Then watch this endpoint. Within 30 seconds PaymentRecoveryJob notices and
### completes the saga — it does not refund, because unwinding a charge costs
### more than finishing the order.
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GET {{orderService}}/api/orders/12
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

### 40. Circuit state for the order-service dependency
GET {{paymentService}}/actuator/circuitbreakers

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### config-server (8888) — Step 6
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
###
### Start it FIRST; the other four refuse to start without it (D17). It needs the
### encryption key, or the dev signing key in the repo cannot be decrypted:
###
###   cd config-server && ENCRYPT_KEY=dev-only-config-server-master-key-do-not-reuse ../mvnw spring-boot:run

@configServer = http://localhost:8888

### 20. Exactly what user-service is handed on startup.
### Note the ORDER of propertySources: user-service-dev.yml, then application-dev.yml, then
### user-service.yml, then application.yml. Profile beats specificity — the shared dev file
### outranks the service's own file, which is the opposite of what everyone assumes.
GET {{configServer}}/user-service/dev

### 21. The same service in prod. `${JWT_SECRET}` and `${DB_URL}` come back UNRESOLVED —
### the config server is told the name of the secret, not the secret (D18).
GET {{configServer}}/user-service/prod

### 22. Each service gets its own database credentials and nobody else's.
GET {{configServer}}/order-service/dev

### 23. Encrypt a value for the repo. Paste the response in as  secret: '{cipher}<response>'
POST {{configServer}}/encrypt
Content-Type: text/plain

some-value-that-should-not-be-readable-in-git

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Changing a value in a running service (Step 6c)
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### 24. /actuator/health is open — a Kubernetes probe carries no token.
GET {{userService}}/actuator/health

### 25. /actuator/refresh is not. Without a token this is 401; with a USER token, 403.
POST {{userService}}/actuator/refresh

### 26. Re-read configuration and rebind. Returns the list of keys that changed.
###
### To see it do something: edit `app.jwt.expiration-minutes` in config-repo/application.yml,
### send this, then log in again (request 2) and decode the token — `exp - iat` follows the new
### value, in the same process, with no restart.
###
### To see it NOT do something: change `app.book-service.url` and refresh order-service. The
### endpoint reports the key as changed and order-service keeps calling the old address, because
### a @FeignClient URL is resolved when the client is built. Updating the Environment is not the
### same as anything changing.
POST {{userService}}/actuator/refresh
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 27. The most common real use: turn on debug logging without restarting the service you are
### trying to observe. Change `logging.level.com.example` in config-repo/application-dev.yml,
### refresh, then read it back here.
GET {{userService}}/actuator/loggers/com.example
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### 28. Where a value actually came from, when four files could have supplied it.
### Credential-looking keys come back as ****** — actuator redacts them.
GET {{userService}}/actuator/env/app.jwt.secret
Authorization: Bearer {{adminToken}}

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 7 — the asynchronous half
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
###
### Two more services, neither of which has an API:
###
###   cd notification-service && ../mvnw spring-boot:run     (8085)
###   cd analytics-service    && ../mvnw spring-boot:run     (8086)
###
### Nothing below calls them. That is the point - they are driven entirely by what
### order-service and payment-service publish, and the only way to see them work is
### to read their logs or their metrics.

@analytics = http://localhost:8086

### 29. Place an order and WATCH THE CLOCK. The confirmation and the analytics tally happen
### after this response has already been returned, in two other processes.
###
### Then stop notification-service, place three more, and start it again: all three
### confirmations appear at once, with the original placedAt. A consumer being down is a
### queue, not an outage.
POST {{orderService}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 7, "quantity": 2 } ] }

### 30. Pay for it. payment-service publishes PaymentCompleted, and notification-service -
### one consumer group, two topics, two event types - logs a RECEIPT as well as the earlier
### CONFIRMATION.
POST {{paymentService}}/api/payments
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{userToken}}

{ "orderId": 25, "amount": 20.00 }

### 31. DLQ depth, as a metric. Zero is the healthy answer and the monitor stays silent for it.
GET {{analytics}}/actuator/metrics/bookstore.dlq.depth

### 32. Health only - these two services expose nothing else, because they have no security
### filter chain and nothing to protect.
GET {{analytics}}/actuator/health

###
### The parts that need the broker directly, not HTTP:
###
###   # who is reading what, and how far behind
###   docker exec bookstore-kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-consumer-groups.sh \
###     --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --describe --all-groups
###
###   # redelivery: republish an OrderPlaced that has already been handled.
###   # Both consumers log "already been counted / confirmed" and neither acts twice.
###   docker exec -i bookstore-kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-producer.sh \
###     --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --topic bookstore.order.placed \
###     --property parse.key=true --property key.separator='|'
###   25|{"orderId":25,"userId":2,"totalPrice":20.00,"items":[],"placedAt":"2026-08-02T18:18:36Z"}
###
###   # a poison message: valid JSON, wrong shape. It goes to the dead letter topic on the
###   # FIRST attempt (a conversion failure is deterministic), and the next good message on
###   # the same partition is processed half a second later.
###   999|{"orderId":999,"userId":1,"totalPrice":"not-a-number","items":[],"placedAt":"2026-08-02T18:18:36Z"}
###
###   # what ended up there
###   docker exec bookstore-kafka /opt/kafka/bin/kafka-console-consumer.sh \
###     --bootstrap-server localhost:9092 --from-beginning \
###     --topic bookstore.order.placed.notification-service.DLT

### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
### Step 8 — the front door
### ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
###
###   cd api-gateway && ../mvnw spring-boot:run      (8080 public, 9090 management)
###
### Everything above this line talks to a service directly, which is how the platform
### looked before Step 8. Everything below goes through one address.

### 33. Log in through the gateway. Public: registering and logging in cannot require
### being logged in.
# @name gatewayLogin
POST {{gateway}}/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{ "username": "shopper", "password": "correct-horse-battery" }

@gwToken = {{gatewayLogin.response.body.token}}

### 34. Browsing the catalogue is public too - an anonymous visitor has to be able to
### shop before deciding to register. No Authorization header at all.
GET {{gateway}}/api/books

### 35. A protected route with no token -> 401 AT THE EDGE. order-service never hears
### about this request: it costs no connection, no thread and no database session.
GET {{gateway}}/api/orders

### 36. The same route with a real token -> 200, forwarded with the token untouched
### plus X-Auth-User / X-Auth-User-Id / X-Auth-Role for downstream logs.
GET {{gateway}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{gwToken}}

### 37. A whole purchase through one address.
POST {{gateway}}/api/orders
Content-Type: application/json
Authorization: Bearer {{gwToken}}

{ "items": [ { "bookId": 7, "quantity": 2 } ] }

### 38. FORGING IDENTITY THROUGH THE GATEWAY -> 401. The gateway strips every inbound
### X-Auth-* before it decides anything, and logs the attempt. Headers a proxy sets
### must be headers a proxy also clears.
GET {{gateway}}/api/orders
X-Auth-User: admin
X-Auth-User-Id: 1
X-Auth-Role: ADMIN

### 39. BYPASSING THE GATEWAY ENTIRELY -> still 401. This is the request that proves
### the services do not trust the edge: they check the signature, so reaching a service
### directly gains an attacker nothing. It is also why the token is forwarded rather
### than replaced by a trusted header.
DELETE {{bookService}}/api/books/1
X-Auth-User: admin
X-Auth-Role: ADMIN

### 40. A CORS preflight to a PROTECTED route -> 200, not 401. A browser sends OPTIONS
### with no Authorization header at all; refusing it would make the real request never
### happen, and the console would say "CORS" with nothing mentioning a token.
OPTIONS {{gateway}}/api/orders
Origin: http://localhost:3000
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: authorization,content-type

### 41. An origin nobody allowed -> 403.
OPTIONS {{gateway}}/api/orders
Origin: https://evil.example.com
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST

### 42. CORS IS NOT ACCESS CONTROL. This request carries no Origin header, so no CORS
### check applies to it at all - and it still needs a valid token, which is the part
### that actually protects anything.
GET {{gateway}}/api/orders
Authorization: Bearer {{gwToken}}

### 43. /actuator/** IS NOT ROUTED. Every route starts /api, so there is no path from
### the public port to any service's actuator - and none to the gateway's own either,
### which is served on 9090. Expect 404.
GET {{gateway}}/actuator/env

### 44. The gateway's own actuator, on the management port. In Step 10 only 8080 is
### named in the Service, so this is unreachable from outside the pod by construction.
GET http://localhost:9090/actuator/gateway/routes
